   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Research Question"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Did Bitcoin behave more like a safe-haven asset or risk asset before, during, and after the COVID-19 pandemic (2017-2025), based on its correlations with the S&P 500, Gold price index, and U.S. Dollar index?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Background and Prior Work"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Over the past decade, cryptocurrencies–especially Bitcoin—have transitioned from niche digital assets into instruments increasingly integrated into global financial markets. As governmental and institutional participation has grown, researchers and investors have become more interested in understanding how Bitcoin interacts and compares with traditional macro assets. Namely: equities, commodities and currencies are subject to being juxtaposed with Bitcoin in consideration of their comparative investment advantages during such an unbridled economic period such as the Covid-19 era. \n",
    "We define the “Covid-19 era” to take place from the year 2017 through 2025, for our purposes. Specifically, the “pre-Covid era” will be the three years before the pandemic’s inception: 2017-2019; the “during-Covid era” will be the three years following its inception including its peak: 2020-2022; and the “post-Covid era” will be the three years following its peak: 2023-2025. Understanding whether Bitcoin behaves more like a speculative technology stock, a safe haven such as gold, or a liquidity sensitive macro asset is important for portfolio construction, risk management, and macroeconomic analysis.\n",
    "\n",
    "Prior research suggests Bitcoin price historically exhibited low correlations with traditional assets before 2020. For example, Baur et al., analyzed Bitcoin's statistical properties and role as a diversification asset and found weak correlations with stocks and gold, concluding that bitcoin behaved more like a speculative investment than a safe haven or hedging tool.<sup><a href=\"#ref1\">1</a></sup> Similarly, Corbet et al. examined spillovers between cryptocurrencies and financial markets and reported limited integration between crypto and traditional assets during Bitcoin's early growth phase.<sup><a href=\"#ref2\">2</a></sup> These findings substantiate the early narrative that Bitcoin could serve as a portfolio diversifier due to its independence from typical macro markets.\n",
    "\n",
    "However, more recent studies suggest that the Covid-19 pandemic marked a structural shift in asset correlations. Conlon and McGee examined Bitcoin’s performance during the March 2020 market crash and found it failed to act as a safe haven. Instead, Bitcoin exhibited increasing co-movement in price with equites during extreme volatility.<sup><a href=\"#ref3\">3</a></sup> In a broader time-varying analysis, Dutta et al. documented dynamic correlations between gold, and stock markets, finding that Bitcoin’s relationship with equities strengthened during periods of market stress.<sup><a href=\"#ref4\">4</a></sup> These findings suggest that correlations involving Bitcoin are not static but depend heavily on macroeconomic regimes.\n",
    "\n",
    "Despite this growing body of literature, relatively few student or open-source data science projects explicitly examine how correlations between Bitcoin and multiple macro assets evolve across distinct time regimes, such as pre-Covid, pandemic, and post-pandemic periods; at least that we were able to find. Our project builds on existing literature by performing a regime-based correlation analysis across several major asset classes–the S&P 500, gold, and the U.S. dollar index–over the period 2017-2025. By combining rolling correlations and period segmentation, this work aims to evaluate whether Bitcoin has structurally transitioned from an uncorrelated alternative currency into a macro-sensitive risk asset.\n",
    "#### References + Citations:\n",
    "1. [^](#cite_ref-1)<a id=\"ref1\"></a>Baur, D. G., Hong, K., & Lee, A. D. (2018). Bitcoin: Medium of exchange or speculative assets? Journal of International Financial Markets, *Institutions and Money.* https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1042443117300720\n",
    "2. [^](#cite_ref-2)<a id=\"ref2\"></a>Corbet, S., Meegan, A., Larkin, C., Lucey, B., & Yarovaya, L. (2018). Exploring the dynamic relationships between cryptocurrencies and other financial assets. *Economics Letters.*\n",
    "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0165176518300041\n",
    "3. [^](#cite_ref-3)<a id=\"ref3\"></a>Conlon, T., & McGee, R. (2020). Safe haven or risky hazard? Bitcoin during the COVID-19 bear market. *Finance Research Letters.*\n",
    "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1544612320304244\n",
    "4. [^](#cite_ref-4)<a id=\"ref4\"></a>Dutta, A., Das, D., Jana, R., & Vo, X. V. (2020). COVID-19 and oil market crash: Revisiting the safe haven property of gold and Bitcoin. *Resources Policy.*\n",
    "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0301420720308485\n",
    "5. <a id=\"ref5\"></a>Fidelity Digital Assets (2023–2024 reports); Coinbase Institutional research blog.\n",
    "6. <a id=\"ref6\"></a>Federal Reserve Economic Data (FRED) and Bloomberg market analyses on BTC–DXY relationships.\n",
    "7. <a id=\"ref7\"></a>Di Wu, (2025). Institutional Adoption and Correlation Dynamics: Bitcoin's Evolving Role in Financial Markets. *arXiv: Quantitative Finance.*\n",
    "https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.09911\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Hypothesis\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "From 2017 to 2025 in the United States, the relationship between price returns of Bitcoin and traditional macro-financial assets—the S&P 500 Index, gold price, and the U.S. Dollar Index (DXY)—changed significantly across the pre-COVID (2017–2019), during-COVID (2020–2022), and post-COVID (2023–2025) periods. Prior to the COVID-19 pandemic, Bitcoin exhibited weak or negligible correlations with equities, gold, and the U.S. dollar, consistent with its characterization as an alternative or speculative asset. During the COVID-19 period, Bitcoin’s correlation with the S&P 500 increased significantly while its correlations with gold and the dollar failed to strengthen in a manner consistent with safe-haven behavior, reflecting heightened integration with risk-on financial markets (i.e. stocks) during periods of systemic stress. In the post-COVID period, these elevated equity correlations persisted or stabilized, suggesting that Bitcoin has structurally transitioned toward behaving as a macro-sensitive risk asset rather than an independent currency. This hypothesis is tested by examining changes in correlation structure and regression coefficients across the three time regimes using daily data.\n"
   ]
  },
