   "source": [
    "### Gold Index Daily Prices\n",
    "\n",
    "This dataset contains daily observations of Gold Futures (GC=F) prices from January 2017 through December 2025. Gold futures are standardized contracts used as a global benchmark for gold price movements. All price values are reported in U.S. dollars (USD) per troy ounce, the standard unit for precious metals trading. The dataset includes six primary variables: Date, Open, High, Low, Close, and Volume.\n",
    "\n",
    "The Open price represents the first transaction price of the trading session, while the High and Low values capture the maximum and minimum prices observed during the day. The Close price reflects the final traded value and is commonly interpreted as the market’s consensus valuation at the end of trading; for example, a Close price of 1,800 indicates that one troy ounce of gold was valued at $1,800 at market close. Volume measures the number of futures contracts traded and serves as an indicator of market participation and liquidity.\n",
    "\n",
    "Gold is widely considered a safe-haven asset and an inflation hedge, making it relevant for analyzing financial market behavior during periods of economic uncertainty, including the COVID-19 pandemic. The dataset’s time span allows for comparison of gold price dynamics across pre-pandemic, pandemic, and recovery periods.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Dataset Concerns and Limitations**\n",
    "\n",
    "This dataset reflects futures market prices rather than physical spot gold prices, which may introduce speculative and contract-roll effects. Financial markets are also closed on weekends and holidays, resulting in missing calendar dates that represent non-trading periods. Additionally, the raw dataset contains formatting artifacts from the Yahoo Finance export process that require preprocessing to obtain a tidy dataset. Finally, gold prices are influenced by macroeconomic and geopolitical factors not directly captured in this dataset, meaning observed relationships should be interpreted as associative rather than causal.\n"
   ]
